    "description": "Press coverage for SaaS founders and companies — building category credibility, where we place clients, and how the process works.",
    "body": "<p>Your prospect has already read your website, checked G2, and searched your name before anyone on your team hears from them.</p>\n<p>Press is what they find in that third step.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-press-works-for-saas\">Why press works for SaaS</h2>\n<p>Software is a trust purchase. Buyers are committing to a vendor relationship, a migration, and a line item their boss will question.</p>\n<p>What reduces that risk is third-party validation — someone other than you saying your approach is credible. A published feature does that in a way no landing page can, because you did not control the platform it appeared on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-most-saas-companies-get-wrong\">What most SaaS companies get wrong</h2>\n<p>They pitch the product.</p>\n<p>Editors do not publish feature announcements. They publish arguments: why the standard approach to a problem is failing, what your usage data reveals about how teams actually work, where your category is heading and why. Your product is the proof, not the subject.</p>\n<p>The companies that get covered consistently are the ones with a point of view.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-we-place-saas-clients\">Where we place SaaS clients</h2>\n<p>Business and technology publications whose readers include operators, founders and buyers — including MSN, Yahoo Finance, Benzinga, Digital Journal, CEO Weekly and NY Weekly.</p>\n<p>We match the outlet to your buyer, not to whichever logo looks best.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-feature-does-for-your-business\">What a feature does for your business</h2>\n<p>A permanent, indexed article that appears in your buyer's research. Something your sales team can send during a deal. Evidence for investors that the market takes you seriously. A credential when you hire.</p>\n<p>For a category-creating product, it is also how you get the category named the way you want it named.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works</h2>\n<p>Share your story, your data and your point of view. We develop the angle, write the piece to editorial standard, and manage placement. You review and revise until it is right, and nothing publishes without your approval.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Should we wait until we have more customers?</h3>\n<p>No. Insight beats scale for editorial purposes. A small company with real data and a clear argument gets published ahead of a larger one with nothing to say.</p>\n<h3>Can press help with enterprise sales?</h3>\n<p>It helps with the part of the deal you never see — the internal conversation where someone asks whether your company is a safe choice. Coverage answers that quietly.</p>\n<h3>What if our product is technical?</h3>\n<p>That is usually an advantage. Technical depth makes a stronger story, as long as the piece explains why it matters to someone outside engineering.</p>",
