    "body": "<p>Someone is deciding whether to work with you right now, and they are doing it by searching your name.</p>\n<p>What they find is your real sales page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-press-works-for-coaches\">Why press works for coaches</h2>\n<p>There is no product to demo and no spec sheet to compare. A client is buying your judgment, which means they are buying their own confidence in you.</p>\n<p>Testimonials help, but everyone has testimonials and everyone knows you chose them. A published feature is different: a publication decided your perspective was worth its readers' attention. That distinction lands, even when nobody articulates it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-most-coaches-get-wrong\">What most coaches get wrong</h2>\n<p>They pitch their story arc — the rock bottom, the turnaround, the method.</p>\n<p>Editors have seen that a thousand times. What they publish is expertise: the specific thing you understand about your clients that nobody else is saying, the pattern you see across hundreds of sessions, the advice that contradicts the standard advice.</p>\n<p>Teach something. Authority follows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-we-place-coaching-clients\">Where we place coaching clients</h2>\n<p>Publications covering business, leadership, wellness and personal development — including CEO Weekly, Women's Journal, NY Weekly, MSN and Digital Journal.</p>\n<p>Which one fits depends on who you coach. An executive coach and a wellness coach need different rooms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-feature-does-for-your-business\">What a feature does for your business</h2>\n<p>A permanent, indexed article that appears when a prospect searches you. Something to put in your bio, your speaker one-sheet, your email signature and your proposals. Proof you are the person you say you are.</p>\n<p>For coaches raising rates, it is often the thing that makes the new number feel obvious.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works</h2>\n<p>Share your perspective and who you serve. We develop the angle, write the piece to editorial standard, and manage placement. You review and revise until it is right, and nothing publishes without your approval.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>I am not well known yet. Is it too early?</h3>\n<p>No. Editors publish ideas, not follower counts. If you work with clients and have learned something real, you have enough.</p>\n<h3>Will this bring me clients directly?</h3>\n<p>Sometimes, but the bigger effect is on conversion — prospects who already found you close more easily when the search results back you up.</p>\n<h3>Can I use the feature in my marketing?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Link it from your site, your bio and your proposals, and describe accurately what it is. That is what makes it useful.</p>",
