    "slug": "entrepreneurs",
    "h1": "PR for Entrepreneurs and Founders",
    "title": "PR for Entrepreneurs and Founders | DNA PR",
    "description": "PR for entrepreneurs and founders: what a founder PR agency does, how it differs from company PR, the stories that work and where we place entrepreneurs.",
    "body": "<p>A PR agency for entrepreneurs gets you, the founder, written about in the publications your customers, investors, partners and future hires read, and makes sure that coverage is what they find when they search your name. Companies are judged by the people who run them. When someone is deciding whether to buy from you, back you or join you, they search the person behind the business as well as the business itself.</p>\n<p>PR for founders makes sure that search turns up something worth reading. This page explains what founder PR involves, how it differs from company PR, the stories that work, where we place entrepreneurs and how to choose an agency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-press-works-for-entrepreneurs\">Why press works for entrepreneurs</h2>\n<p>Entrepreneurs carry their company's reputation, especially in the early years. A customer weighing a new supplier, an investor reading a deck or a senior hire considering an offer will form a view of you long before they meet you.</p>\n<p>A founder with a public profile finds doors open more easily: speaking invitations, partnership conversations and introductions that land warmly rather than cold.</p>\n<p>Founder coverage is also portable. Your company may change direction or be sold. Coverage about you as an entrepreneur stays with you into whatever you build next.</p>\n<h2 id=\"founder-pr-versus-company-pr\">Founder PR versus company PR</h2>\n<p>Company PR is about the product, the launch and the market. Founder PR is about judgement: what you have learned, what you believe about your industry, and why people should trust you to lead. Good campaigns do both, but the pieces are written differently and are often placed in different titles.</p>\n<p>If your company is at an early, investor-backed stage, our page on <a href=\"/pr-for/startups\">PR for startups</a> covers the company side. This page focuses on you.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-pr-agency-for-founders-does\">What a PR agency for founders does</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Finds your story.</strong> Not your CV, but the turning points: the problem that made you start, the decision that nearly sank the business, the belief that sets you apart.</li><li><strong>Develops your point of view.</strong> A clear position on your industry that a reader can agree or disagree with, which is what makes a profile worth reading.</li><li><strong>Writes it to the publication's standard.</strong> In your voice, so it sounds like you rather than a press office.</li><li><strong>Places it.</strong> In business and entrepreneurship titles your audience reads, managed through to a live link.</li><li><strong>Builds your search results.</strong> Several features across different titles, so a search for your name shows a consistent picture.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-stories-that-work-for-entrepreneurs\">The stories that work for entrepreneurs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>The honest origin story:</strong> why you started, told with the doubts and false starts left in.</li><li><strong>The lesson from failure:</strong> what went wrong, what you changed, and what other founders can take from it.</li><li><strong>The industry view:</strong> the assumption everyone in your market makes that you think is wrong.</li><li><strong>The operating insight:</strong> how you hire, spend your time, run your week or make decisions under pressure.</li><li><strong>The second act:</strong> for serial entrepreneurs, what you are doing differently this time and why.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"where-we-place-entrepreneurs\">Where we place entrepreneurs</h2>\n<p>We work across business and entrepreneurship publications whose readers include founders, operators and investors, including <a href=\"/get-featured-in/ceo-weekly\">CEO Weekly</a>, <a href=\"/get-featured-in/entrepreneur-today\">Entrepreneur Today</a>, <a href=\"/get-featured-in/success-magazine\">Success Magazine</a>, <a href=\"/get-featured-in/us-insider\">US Insider</a>, <a href=\"/get-featured-in/grit-daily\">Grit Daily</a>, <a href=\"/get-featured-in/ny-weekly\">NY Weekly</a>, <a href=\"/get-featured-in/yahoo-finance\">Yahoo Finance</a> and <a href=\"/get-featured-in/msn\">MSN</a>.</p>\n<p>The right titles depend on your goal. A founder raising capital needs different coverage from one building a speaking career or selling to other business owners. We tell you where we are pitching and why before anything is written.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-choose-a-pr-agency-for-entrepreneurs\">How to choose a PR agency for entrepreneurs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Do they ask about you, or only your company?</strong> Founder PR starts with your story and your views. An agency that only wants your product brief is selling company PR.</li><li><strong>Which publications, specifically?</strong> A vague answer usually means a vague result.</li><li><strong>Will it sound like me?</strong> Ask how they capture your voice, and whether you approve every word before it goes live.</li><li><strong>What are they promising?</strong> Be careful with certainty about staff-written titles. Our guide on <a href=\"/guides/how-to-vet-a-pr-agency\">how to vet a PR agency</a> explains what to check.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-pr-for-entrepreneurs-costs\">What PR for entrepreneurs costs</h2>\n<p>It depends on the publications, how many features you need and over what period. We quote each founder individually rather than selling a fixed package. For a full picture of how agencies charge, see <a href=\"/guides/how-much-does-a-pr-agency-cost\">how much a PR agency costs</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works</h2>\n<p>Tell us your story and where you want to be seen. We develop the angle, write the piece to the publication's standard and manage the placement. You review and revise until it is right, and nothing publishes without your approval. Our guide on <a href=\"/guides/how-to-build-a-personal-brand-with-press\">building a personal brand with press</a> explains how founder coverage fits into a longer plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What does a PR agency for founders do?</h3>\n<p>It finds the story in your career and your views, writes it in your voice to the standard of the publication, and places it where customers, investors and future hires will see it when they search your name.</p>\n<h3>Should an entrepreneur do personal PR or company PR first?</h3>\n<p>It depends on what people search. In the early years most people look up the founder, so personal coverage often comes first and supports the company later.</p>\n<h3>Do I need to be well known already to get press?</h3>\n<p>No. Most founders start with no coverage at all. What matters is a clear story and a point of view, not existing fame.</p>",
    "toc": [
      { "id": "why-press-works-for-entrepreneurs", "text": "Why press works for entrepreneurs" },
      { "id": "founder-pr-versus-company-pr", "text": "Founder PR versus company PR" },
      { "id": "what-a-pr-agency-for-founders-does", "text": "What a PR agency for founders does" },
      { "id": "the-stories-that-work-for-entrepreneurs", "text": "The stories that work for entrepreneurs" },
      { "id": "where-we-place-entrepreneurs", "text": "Where we place entrepreneurs" },
      { "id": "how-to-choose-a-pr-agency-for-entrepreneurs", "text": "How to choose a PR agency for entrepreneurs" },
      { "id": "what-pr-for-entrepreneurs-costs", "text": "What PR for entrepreneurs costs" },
      { "id": "how-it-works", "text": "How it works" },
      { "id": "frequently-asked-questions", "text": "Frequently asked questions" }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "PR for entrepreneurs shapes what customers, investors and hires find when they search the founder, not only the company.",
      "Founder PR is built on judgement and point of view: origin stories, lessons from failure and industry views.",
      "Every piece is written in your voice to the publication's standard, and you approve every word."
    ]
